// Bytes through, unchanged — and the file that has now driven three versions of `In`.
//
// The shipped `cat` is `i32 cat(Core core, Cli cli, Fs fs, Args a)`. Here it is
// `cat(Out out, In in, Files files, Args a)`: no clock, no randomness, no sockets, no spawning. That
// is four capabilities becoming four *narrower* capabilities rather than four becoming two, which is
// the honest version of the projection argument for an applet that genuinely reads files.
//
// ## Three versions of the same complaint, and only the third one fixed anything
//
// **First**, this found `In` and `Socket` disagreeing about how a read ends. `Socket.recv` answered a
// `Read` sum — `Data`, `End`, `Failed` — and `In.read` answered `Ticket<u8[]>`, whose only way to say
// *end* was an empty array. A sentinel drawn from the value's own range, the third instance of a
// shape `unicode`'s `decode` and `Socket` had already shown between them. The fix looked obvious:
// give `In` a `Read` too.
//
// **Second**, `Files.open` arrived. The audit of all 62 host capabilities found the shipped
// `openInput`/`readChunk`/`closeFeed` had no projection at all, so a file was read whole — the
// entire contents in memory, in the one command whose job is arbitrarily large input. With
// `Files.open` answering an `AsyncGenerator<Bytes, …>` the file half became a `try await for` and
// buffers nothing. And that made this file *worse*: two loops for one behaviour, one over a
// generator and one testing a sentinel, in the program that exists to treat the two alike.
//
// **Third**, `In` became a stream, and the sentinel did not get a better spelling — it stopped
// existing. Below there is one `copy`, one call site per source, and `-` on the command line is an
// operand like any other. The version of this file that had `pump` and a `for` loop is gone rather
// than fixed, which is what a redesign is supposed to look like.
//
// **So the question was never how a capability says *end*.** It was that `In` was not a stream in a
// system that has them, and *sum versus sentinel* was a question about how to spell something that
// should not have been there. Worth writing down because both earlier answers were reasonable and
// both were arguing inside the wrong frame.

import { Result, Bytes, AsyncGenerator } from "core";
import { Out, In, Files, NotGranted } from "std";
import { Args } from "@/packages/box/src/lib/args.wac";

/**
 * Every operand, and `-` for standard input.
 *
 * `-` is not special here beyond choosing a source, which is the whole of what the third version
 * bought: the two sources have one type, so `cat a - b` interleaves a file, a pipe and a file
 * through the same loop, and nothing in the body knows which is which.
 *
 * With no operands it is exactly `cat -`, so that case is a default rather than a branch.
 */
export async i32 cat(Out out, In in, Files files, Args a) {
  // No operands is `cat -`, and this line is a branch only because there is no way to write the
  // one-element collection that would make it a default. See below.
  if (a.operands.len() == 0) {
    await copy(out, in.stream());
    return 0;
  }

  i32 status = 0;
  for (string name in a.operands) {
    if (name == "-") {
      await copy(out, in.stream());
      continue;
    }
    match (files.open(name)) {
      Ok(source): { await copy(out, source); }
      Err(_): {
        _ = out.writeErr("cat: \{name}: cannot read\n".toBytes());
        status = 1;
      }
    }
  }
  return status;
}

/** One source to standard output. */
async void copy(Out out, AsyncGenerator<Bytes, Result<void, NotGranted>> source) {
  try await for (Bytes chunk in source) { _ = out.write(chunk); }
}

// ## What could not be written
//
// **`copy` swallows the source's failure and there is nowhere to put it.** `try await for`
// propagates the generator's `Err` as *this* function's return, and `copy` returns `void`, so a read
// that fails halfway through a file is indistinguishable from one that ended. Making it
// `async Result<void, NotGranted>` is correct and moves the problem: the caller then has a
// per-operand failure to report, which is right, and `cat` already reports one for *cannot open*.
// Two failures for one operand, and the shipped `cat` has the same two and prints the same sentence
// for both, so this is not a regression — it is the thing a `Result` would let this file say for the
// first time and the reason it is not written is that `NotGranted` is the wrong type for *the pipe
// broke*. Which is the entry below.
//
// **The generator's failure type is `Result<void, NotGranted>` for both sources and should not be.**
// A file's read can fail because the grant was refused, and so can standard input's — but a file can
// also be a directory, be removed underneath, or hit a device error, and a pipe can be closed by the
// other end. `Fault` in `@/packages/fs` is the union that exists for exactly this and `std` cannot
// use it, because `std` is below the packages. So the capability answers the one failure it can
// name from where it sits, and the interesting failures have no arm.
//
// **The empty-operands case is a branch and wants to be a default**, and this is where the **list
// literal** finally gets a case. The line wants to be
//
//     Vec<string> names = a.operands.len() == 0 ? ["-"] : a.operands;
//
// after which `-` is an operand like any other and the body has one shape for all three of *a file*,
// *a pipe*, and *no arguments at all*. What stops it is that there is no way to build a one-element
// collection: `Vec` has `create()` and `push`, so it takes two statements and a name, and a
// `Vec.of(T)` helper cannot be written usefully because **there are no variadics** — `of` would need
// one overload per arity, which is the argument for a literal restated as a library.
//
// So `["-"]` is a real case for a construct that had none, and it is a small one: what it buys here
// is deleting a four-line branch. Worth recording as *found by writing something* rather than as an
// argument, because the construct has been in `../../../GRAMMAR.ebnf` for nine days on the strength
// of appearing on a page, and this is the first line anywhere that wanted it.
